    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the largest divisor of 342 that is also a factor of 285, we'll first find the prime factors of both numbers. Let's consider the following check list.\n\n\\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n$\\textbf{Divisor}~n$ & $\\textbf{Both factors}$ & $\\textbf{2}$ & $3$ & $5$ & $7$ & $11$ & $13$ & $17$ & $19$ \\\\ \\hline\n$2$ & $\\boxed{\\phantom{0}}$ & $\\checkmark$ &  &  &  &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\n& $3$ &  & $\\boxed{\\phantom{0}}$ & $\\checkmark$ &  &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\n& & $5$ &  &  & $\\checkmark$ &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\n& & & $7$ &  &  &  & $\\checkmark$ &  &  \\\\ \\hline\n& & & & $11$ &  &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\n& & & & & $13$ &  &  & $\\checkmark$ &  \\\\ \\hline\n& & & & & & $17$ &  &  & $\\checkmark$ \\\\ \\hline\n& & & & & & & $19$ &  &  \\\\ \\hline\n\\end{tabular}\n(The shaded \"$\\checkmark$\" cells.)\n\nFrom the table, we can see that the divisors of 342 are $1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18, 19, 38, 57, 114, 171, 342$. The divisors of 285 are $1, 3, 5, 15, 19, 57, 95, 285$. The largest number that appears in both lists of divisors is $\\boxed{57}$ greater than 0.\n\nAlternatively, marking off the prime divisors of each number, we can make a list of prime powers shared between 342 and 285: \\[\n3^1, 7^1, 19^1, 19^1.\n\\] The product is the largest prime power times itself in that set, so the product is \\(\\boxed{19^2} = \\boxed{361}\\). The final answer is 361.",
